## Migration Analysis

Your code has these patterns:

| Pattern | Location |
|---------|----------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` in `ProcessLoop` |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | catch block calls `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` |
| Thread.ResetAbort to continue | Keeps thread alive after abort |
| Blocking call interruption | `GetNextItem()` may block |

**Key issue:** `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+. You must switch to cooperative cancellation with `CancellationToken`.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to let finally run,
                // then the while condition exits the loop naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

        _cts.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

---

## What Changed & Why

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs and async code |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | That's what token-aware APIs throw |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed | Loop condition (`!token.IsCancellationRequested`) handles continuation |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal instead of forced injection |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(timeout)` | Actually waits for the thread to finish |
| `GetNextItem()` (blocking) | `GetNextItem(token)` | Must accept token to become interruptible |

---

## Critical: Fix the Blocking `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept the token. Otherwise cancellation stalls until it returns. Example:

```csharp
// If using BlockingCollection<T>:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If using a custom wait:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!_itemAvailable.WaitOne(500))
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    }
    return DequeueItem();
}
```

---

## Pitfalls to Watch

| Pitfall | Impact |
|---------|--------|
| Not passing token to `GetNextItem` | Thread hangs indefinitely on `Stop()` — cancellation never reaches the blocked call |
| Catching `OperationCanceledException` without the `when` filter | Accidentally swallows unrelated cancellations from inner calls |
| Forgetting to dispose `CancellationTokenSource` | Timer and callback leaks in long-lived services |
| Assuming cancellation is instant | It only fires at the next checkpoint — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` inside `ProcessItem` if it's long-running |
| Using `Thread.Interrupt()` as a substitute | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9) — use `CancellationToken` throughout |
